WEBVTT

00:00:00.222 --> 00:00:04.394
The replay passed, and the response looked
right. But users waited longer for the

00:00:04.394 --> 00:00:08.668
answer to start. Here's how to find that
slowdown in Speedscale and catch it on the

00:00:08.668 --> 00:00:09.286
next replay.

00:00:09.694 --> 00:00:15.095
Open the replay report and select the LLM
tab. Start with p95 time to first token.

00:00:15.095 --> 00:00:19.969
About 95 percent of streams that produced
output started within that time.

00:00:20.389 --> 00:00:24.620
The top number is from the replay. If the
capture included frame timing, the

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:28.907
recorded value appears underneath. Here,
the first token is arriving about 20

00:00:28.907 --> 00:00:29.687
percent later.

00:00:30.111 --> 00:00:34.211
Next, check inter-token latency. It
measures the average gap per token once

00:00:34.211 --> 00:00:38.748
output starts. A higher number means the
answer arrives more slowly after the first

00:00:38.748 --> 00:00:39.076
token.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.784
Open a streamed response and select
Stream. The timeline shows when each frame

00:00:43.784 --> 00:00:48.124
arrived. The first frame may just identify
the assistant, while the first-token

00:00:48.124 --> 00:00:50.321
marker shows when output actually began.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:54.745
The last-output marker shows when the
model stopped producing output. Use these

00:00:54.745 --> 00:00:58.438
two markers to see whether the wait is at
the start or during the stream.

00:00:58.861 --> 00:01:04.338
To catch this on the next run, add an LLM
TTFT p95 goal to your test config. Set the

00:01:04.338 --> 00:01:09.423
comparison to Too High and enter 800
milliseconds. Leave the location blank so

00:01:09.423 --> 00:01:11.835
the goal applies to the whole replay.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:16.580
Now replay the same recorded prompts
against your model server. In this lab, we

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:20.854
start with the baseline server, then run
the same test config against a slower

00:01:20.854 --> 00:01:21.238
server.

00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:25.942
The slower server still passes the
response checks, but its p95 time to first

00:01:25.942 --> 00:01:29.441
token exceeds the limit. Check the missed
goal before you ship.

00:01:29.861 --> 00:01:34.392
Try the LLM streaming lab to run both
versions and set a latency limit for your

00:01:34.392 --> 00:01:35.080
own service.
